From 6480b2353663439b0d208406104da2761256d09f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Urho Laurinen <urho.laurinen@siroic.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] gptel: Add a generic properties plist to gptel-tool

`gptel-tool' has no room for metadata that gptel itself does not
interpret, so extensions that need to annotate a tool (how to render its
output, which UI affordance to attach, a category of their own) have to
maintain a parallel table keyed on the tool name, or resort to
defadvice.

Add a `properties' slot -- a plain plist, accepted by the constructor
and never read by gptel -- as the documented place for such extension
data.

No test: the change adds an inert struct slot that gptel never reads; there is no behaviour to pin beyond the constructor accepting the keyword.
---
diff --git a/gptel-request.el b/gptel-request.el
index fc5115c..914799b 100644
--- a/gptel-request.el
+++ b/gptel-request.el
@@ -1497,6 +1497,7 @@ a tool, use `gptel-make-tool', which see."
                           (:constructor gptel--make-tool-internal
                                         (&key function name description args
                                               async category confirm include
+                                              properties
                                               &allow-other-keys))
                           (:copier gptel--copy-tool))
   "Struct to specify tools for LLMs to run.
@@ -1516,7 +1517,8 @@ feed the LLM the results.  You can add tools via
   (include t :type (choice (const :tag "Include call and result" t)
                            (const :tag "Include call only, no result" call)
                            (const :tag "Exclude" nil))
-           :documentation "Include tool call in buffer?"))
+           :documentation "Include tool call in buffer?")
+  (properties nil :type list :documentation "Plist of extension properties not interpreted by gptel itself (e.g. :org-output)."))
 
 (defun gptel--preprocess-tool-args (spec)
   "Convert symbol :type values in tool SPEC to strings destructively."
